110	Quantum soundness of a version of the classical low-degree test for polynomials of low \emph{total} degree was claimed by Natarajan and Vidick, building on an analysis of a three-prover version of the test by Vidick; there is a known gap in the soundness analysis of that test, and it is not used here. Instead, the construction relies on the low \emph{individual} degree test, whose quantum soundness is proved in~\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree} and generalized in~\cite{Ji2021Quantum}. The statement below is the form most directly useful in what follows.
111	
112	% The paper states this result in a theorem environment with the label lem:ld-soundness.
113	% We keep both the environment and the label verbatim for cross-reference fidelity.
114	\begin{theorem}[Quantum soundness of the simultaneous classical low individual degree test]\label{lem:ld-soundness}
115		\uses{def:ld-game, def:ld-question-distribution, def:ld-win-predicate, def:ld-meas, def:projective-strategy-general, def:tensor-product-value, def:povm-distance, def:bracket}
116		There exists a function
117		\[
118			\delta_{\mathrm{ld}}(\eps, q, m, d, k) = a\,(dmk)^a \bigl(\eps^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd}\bigr)
119		\]
120		for universal constants $a \ge 1$ and $0 < b \le 1$ such that the following holds. For all $\eps > 0$ and every parameter tuple $\mathsf{ldparams} = (q,m,d,k)$ as in Definition~\ref{def:ld-game} (so $q$ is an admissible field size and $m$ divides $q$), and for all projective strategies $(\psi, A, B)$ that succeed with probability at least $1-\eps$ in the game $\mathcal G^{\mathrm{ld}}_{\mathsf{ldparams}}$, there exist measurements
121		\[
122			G^w \in \mathrm{PolyMeas}(m,q,d,k)
123		\]
124		on $\hilb_w$ for $w \in \{\mathrm A,\mathrm B\}$, where by slight abuse of notation $m$, $q$, $d$ denote the constant tuples $(m,\ldots,m)$, $(q,\ldots,q)$, $(d,\ldots,d)$ of length $k$, such that
125		\begin{align*}
126			A^{\mathsf{Point},\, u}_{a_1,\ldots,a_k} \ot I_{\mathrm B}
127			&\approx_{\delta_{\mathrm{ld}}}
128			I_{\mathrm A} \ot G^{\mathrm B}_{[(g_1(u),\ldots,g_k(u)) = (a_1,\ldots,a_k)]}\;,\\
129			G^{\mathrm A}_{[(g_1(u),\ldots,g_k(u)) = (a_1,\ldots,a_k)]} \ot I_{\mathrm B}
130			&\approx_{\delta_{\mathrm{ld}}}
131			I_{\mathrm A} \ot B^{\mathsf{Point},\, u}_{a_1,\ldots,a_k}\;,\\
132			G^{\mathrm A}_{g_1,\ldots,g_k} \ot I_{\mathrm B}
133			&\approx_{\delta_{\mathrm{ld}}}
134			I_{\mathrm A} \ot G^{\mathrm B}_{g_1,\ldots,g_k}\;,
135		\end{align*}
136		where $\delta_{\mathrm{ld}} = \delta_{\mathrm{ld}}(\eps,q,m,d,k)$, all three approximations are with respect to the state $\ket{\psi}$, and the bracket notation for post-processed outcomes is that of Definition~\ref{def:bracket}. The first two approximations hold on average over the uniform distribution on $u \in \F_q^m$; no question distribution is associated with the third.
137	\end{theorem}
138	\begin{proof}
139		\uses{def:ld-question-distribution, def:ld-win-predicate, def:ld-meas, def:povm-distance}
140		Let $\Sigma = \F_q$ and let $\code \subseteq \Sigma^q$ be the Reed--Solomon code of degree $d$: the set of all $(p(x_1),\ldots,p(x_q))$ where $p\colon \F_q \to \F_q$ is a univariate polynomial of degree at most $d$ and $x_1,\ldots,x_q$ enumerate $\F_q$. We may assume $d < q$. In the opposite regime the statement is immediate: for $k \ge 1$ and $d \ge q$ the prefactor gives $\delta_{\mathrm{ld}} \ge a\,(dmk)^a\, q^{-b} \ge q^{a-b} \ge 1$, while the quantity bounded in Definition~\ref{def:povm-distance} never exceeds $2$ for families of POVM elements, so the three approximations hold for an arbitrary choice of $G^w \in \mathrm{PolyMeas}(m,q,d,k)$; and for $k = 0$ every answer tuple is empty, all measurements involved are single-outcome, and the approximations hold with error zero. For $d < q$, evaluation is injective on polynomials of degree at most $d$ and a nonzero polynomial of degree at most $d$ vanishes at no more than $d$ of the $q$ points, so $\code$ is a linear $[q,\, d+1,\, q-d]_\Sigma$ code.
141		% The paper prints the parameters as [q, d+1, n-d+1] with an undefined n. With the
142		% intended value n = q the printed distance q-d+1 exceeds the true minimum distance
143		% q-d of the degree-d evaluation code, and the dimension claim d+1 requires d < q.
144		% Both points are corrected above; the regime d >= q, which the parameter tuple of
145		% Definition def:ld-game permits, is handled by the triviality argument.
146		The two-prover tensor-code test for the code $\code^{\ot m}$, as described in Figures~1 and~2 of~\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, coincides with the game $\mathcal G^{\mathrm{ld}}$ with parameters $\mathsf{ldparams}$ and $k=1$: the axis-parallel line-versus-point test is their axis-parallel lines test, with the roles of the two players chosen uniformly at random, which is consistent with the two-prover variant of the tensor-code test from Section~4.1 there; the diagonal line-versus-point test is their subcube commutation test, again with the added symmetrization between the players; and the consistency test is their synchronicity test. Theorem~4.7 of~\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, instantiated with its internal parameter $k$ chosen as $m^3 d$ (this parameter is unrelated to the simultaneity parameter $k$ of $\mathsf{ldparams}$), therefore yields the present statement for $k = 1$. The case of general $k$ follows by a standard reduction, following exactly the derivation of Theorem~4.43 from Theorem~4.40 in~\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Quantum soundness of the low individual degree test itself is the result of~\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}; Remark~\ref{rem:ld-soundness-provider} relates the present statement to Theorem~\ref{thm:main-formal}, which proves that result.
147	\end{proof}
